  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday through next Wednesday)\nIssued at 1155 PM CDT Wed Sep 9 2026\n\nThis weekend is most likely to start off with a broad west to east\nflow across the southern plains. The panhandles will likely stay\nbroadly on the northern fringes of a high pressure system that is\nhelping setup this flow. This will allow a small scale weather \nsystem to pass across the panhandles bringing a low chance for \nrain showers and thunderstorms favoring the eastern panhandles. \nWith the panhandles under the high pressure the temperatures will \nlikely remain hot with highs in the 90s to 100s. \n\nThis flow will then begin to shift on Sunday as a larger scale \nweather system starts to deepen across western CONUS on Sunday.\nThis will shift the flow to be more southwesterly with the\npanhandles still under the northwestern portion of the high \npressure system. This will still allow for a small scale weather \nsystem passage over the panhandles bringing another low chance for\nrain showers and thunderstorms favoring the western panhandles.\nthe bigger ticket item is the shifting pattern will bring a\nstrengthening pressure gradient to the southern plains. This will\nmake it more likely for stronger winds to blow across the\npanhandles. This does introduce the threat of elevated to even low\nend critical fire weather conditions across portions of the\npanhandles on Sunday. It will still likely remain hot across the\npanhandles with highs in the 90s to 100s. \n\nMonday through Wednesday the large scale weather system passes\nacross the northern and southern plains passing a cold front into\nthe southern plains. This would bring an increase in dynamics and\ninstability to the panhandles for the first half of next work \nweek. The passage of the weather system seems more likely than not\nto draw up copious amounts of gulf moisture in the southern \nplains including the panhandles. The combination of the moisture \nand weather system can kick off a period of active weather across \nthe panhandles. Currently there remains a low to medium chance for\nrain showers and thunderstorms mainly on Monday and Tuesday. \nWednesday is looking less favorable with only a low chance as the \nweather system is more likely than not to have pushed off further \nto the east. Still any prospect of rain is welcome in the \npanhandles as we are still in drought conditions.",
